| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation | FIX | **Fig 6.23 values: all verified.** Panel A ends at about 144, 128, 114, 108 and 105%. A hump peaks at about 113% near week 8.8, then falls to the lowest end value; by end position and label order (maximum strength, absolute strength, speed) that is speed, and the entry's "by that reading" caveat is fair. Panel B lows run from about 83% to 96% at weeks 4-5. Curves cross back above 100% at about weeks 7-9 and end at about 157, 142-143, 129 (peak of about 130-131 near week 16.5), 120 and 114%. Dashed lines sit at weeks 6 and 12. Which curve dips deepest cannot be settled because the curves cross at weeks 7-9, as the entry says. **Problem 1:** When NOT has "Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block". This compares dip duration with block length, which are different quantities. The text on the same page says the concentrated volume sits "in the first half of the training phase" (PDF 360 image), and a dip through weeks 7-9 of 18 is that first half. Indicators are expected to stay low until volume drops. **Problem 2:** The Issurin (2016) critique is stated accurately: insufficient evidence for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, with fitness gains but no reported effect on team practice or competition (p.6), and better suited to narrow target-quality sports (p.11), all as in the vault brief. But the entry does not say whose model is being criticised; per the brief, it is Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R. **Problem 3:** "Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope". PDF 360 says the method "was devised for highly qualified athletes"; it does not forbid it for medium-level athletes. And 6.9.5 (`claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`, PDF 371) says "both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification". Checked and correct: all the PDF 360-361 quotes, Komarova 1984 ("he errs"), and baseline line 105 (Inferred, "Not claimed in the interview"). `contested` stands, for the Issurin reason. | (1) Replace the Fig 6.23 "figure against text" sentence with: "Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's 'concentrated in the first half of the training phase' (PDF 360). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page. Its x-axis ends at 18 weeks; the text's period is 18-22 weeks." (2) Change "Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence ..." to "Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model ...". (3) Replace the last sentence with: "The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 they say both forms work at medium qualification (PDF 371), so it is not required there." Add the reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]. |
